    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d388">

                <pb facs="0592" n="546" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg546"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/15716½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        President Wilson
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-08-07">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 7, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: I received your telegram
                    of the 6th in regard to the effect of Villa’s arbitrary actions upon the work of
                    the conference. The importance of it seems to be that as the preliminary <pb
                        facs="0593" n="547" xml:id="pg547"/> step was to secure a compromise between
                    factions, it would very much embarrass that plan if Villa should prove himself
                    to be unworthy of consideration by his continued acts of aggression against
                    foreigners.</p>
                <p>Fortunately, however, the situation is much clearer this morning. A despatch from
                        Carothers<anchor n="14" xml:id="d388-f1"/> states that Villa will remain at
                    El Paso until he has had an interview with General Scott and that the meeting of
                    miners, which he had summoned at Chihuahua, would be postponed until after such
                    interview. Secretary Houston has also consented to the establishment under
                    military decree of cattle inspection upon the border. This will relieve, to a
                    considerable measure, I believe, Villa’s desperate financial situation which has
                    induced his arbitrary conduct.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
